import{_ as o,o as a,c as t,a6 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BUF_B7sW.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"JAX host preparation","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"jax-host.md","filePath":"jax-host.md","lastUpdated":null}'),d={name:"jax-host.md"};function r(c,e,n,i,l,p){return a(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="JAX-host-preparation" tabindex="-1">JAX host preparation <a class="header-anchor" href="#JAX-host-preparation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;JAX host preparation {#JAX-host-preparation}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Companion to <a href="./jax">JAX source</a>: the data-only Julia that <code>prepare_data</code> runs on the host, once per dataset.</p><h2 id="Data-only-functions" tabindex="-1">Data-only functions <a class="header-anchor" href="#Data-only-functions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Data-only functions {#Data-only-functions}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Host <code>findfirst(predicate, vector)</code> returns the first matching one-based index or <code>nothing</code>. Captured predicates (<code>isequal(label)</code>, <code>Base.Fix1</code>, <code>Base.Fix2</code>) work in positional kernels too: <code>plate(values) do value; Int(findfirst(isequal(value), levels)); end</code>. Labels and vector lengths remain runtime inputs.</p><p>RK 0.1.286 adds <code>findall(vector)</code>/<code>findall(predicate, vector)</code>: every matching one-based position, possibly empty. Predicates run in order and return <code>Bool</code>. <code>Dict(zip(keys, values))</code>, including <code>eachindex(rows)</code> values, supports indexing, <code>get</code> and <code>haskey</code> in host plates. Zip is reusable and stops at its shortest input. Repeated keys keep their last value; equality follows <code>isequal</code> (NaNs, signed zero). These operations also translate in data-only <code>definitions</code>.</p><p>Host preparation also orders vectors of numbers, strings or tuples of them as Julia&#39;s <code>isless</code> does (-0.0 before 0.0, every NaN last): <code>sort</code>, <code>unique</code> (and <code>sort!</code>, <code>unique!</code> of a list built with <code>push!</code>), <code>searchsortedfirst</code> and <code>searchsortedlast</code> (one-based), and <code>sortperm(v)</code>, whose one-based positions keep equal values in ascending position. The two searches look for a number or a string, not a tuple. <code>invperm(p)</code> of a vector of integers is the inverse permutation and raises <code>ArgumentError: argument is not a permutation</code> as Julia does. A data-only definition such as <code>pos = invperm(vcat(rows_a, rows_b))</code> therefore runs in <code>prepare_data</code>. <code>sort</code>, <code>sort!</code> and <code>sortperm</code> take <code>by</code> as a projection onto tuple fields (<code>by = r -&gt; (r[2], r[1])</code>); any other <code>by</code>, and <code>rev</code> and <code>lt</code>, have no translation. In the JAX functions these calls have none either: a recipe that also reads a parameter (<code>theta[sortperm(rows)]</code>) raises <code>JAXEmissionError</code> naming the function, so give the permutation a definition of its own that reads data alone.</p><p><code>collect(zip(a, b))</code> is a vector of tuples whose fields keep their types: an integer label beside a float stays an integer. Tuples are dictionary keys, also in a generator (<code>Dict(key =&gt; i for (i, key) in enumerate(keys))</code>) and a broadcast lookup (<code>getindex.(Ref(lookup), keys)</code>); a <code>Set</code> takes a generator too. <code>all(predicate, values)</code> and <code>any(predicate, values)</code> call a <code>Bool</code> predicate in order and stop at the first element that decides the result. <code>==</code> and <code>!=</code> of two numerical arrays compare their shapes and values, integers exactly.</p><p>Host preparation keeps ordinary data-only Julia as written (RK 0.1.278). Typed comprehensions convert each element as <code>convert</code> does, so <code>Int[findfirst(==(k), levels) for k in keys]</code> raises when a key is absent; nested generators keep Julia&#39;s clause order. <code>push!</code> and <code>append!</code> grow any local vector, which becomes a fresh Python list, so an input is never written. <code>sizehint!</code>, <code>view</code>, <code>@view</code> and <code>@views</code> read as Julia&#39;s. An index that is <code>findfirst</code>&#39;s result raises <code>ArgumentError: invalid index: nothing of type Nothing</code> when it is <code>nothing</code>. <code>issorted</code> and a scalar <code>isless</code> follow the <code>isless</code> order of <code>sort</code>. A <code>Set</code> holds <code>isequal</code> keys and is never iterated (Julia&#39;s hash order). <code>(; a, b) = nt</code> binds fields by name; an anonymous function is called where host code calls it (<code>filter!</code>).</p><p><code>repeat(A, counts...)</code> tiles <code>A</code> <code>counts[d]</code> times along axis <code>d</code>, as Julia&#39;s <code>repeat(A; outer = counts)</code> does, and <code>repeat(A; inner, outer)</code> first repeats each entry <code>inner[d]</code> times along axis <code>d</code>; each keyword takes an integer or a tuple of them. Counts past <code>A</code>&#39;s rank add trailing axes, as Julia&#39;s do (<code>repeat(v, 1, k)</code> of a vector is a matrix with <code>k</code> columns). Shapes are static while JAX traces, so the JAX functions need static counts (literals, or integers static to <code>jax.jit</code>); a count of data is computed in <code>prepare_data</code> when the recipe reads data alone. A categorical model&#39;s prior scale per coefficient term, repeated once per non-reference category (<code>repeat([intercept_scale, slope_scale], 1, k)</code>), therefore runs in <code>prepare_data</code>, and one module serves every category count. Julia&#39;s argument checks hold: a negative count, or with <code>inner</code> a count list shorter than <code>A</code>&#39;s rank, raises with Julia&#39;s <code>ArgumentError</code> message. A recipe that repeats by a count the functions trace raises <code>JAXEmissionError</code> naming the recipe; a padded (ragged) value has no <code>repeat</code> translation.</p><p>A <code>Bool</code> array index (a <code>BitVector</code> or <code>Vector{Bool}</code>, such as <code>table[table[:, 2] .!= 1, 4]</code> or <code>values[present]</code>) selects the entries where it is true, as Julia&#39;s logical indexing does; it is not shifted as one-based positions. A mask of the array&#39;s whole shape (<code>A[A .&gt; 0]</code>) selects in column-major order. <code>prepare_data</code> and other host code use a NumPy boolean mask, which fails on a mask of another length as Julia&#39;s <code>BoundsError</code> does; a list built with <code>push!</code> keeps the entries whose mask entry is true. In device code the count of selected entries depends on data, so the gather is padded to the mask&#39;s length: the selected entries come first, padding repeats the last of them, and reductions, <code>length</code> and broadcasts mask the padding as they do for a ragged row. A write through a mask writes only the selected entries. A device mask beside another kept dimension (<code>A[mask, :]</code>) and a mask of the groups of a ragged value raise <code>JAXEmissionError</code>, as does a single <code>Bool</code> index into an array, which Julia refuses.</p><h2 id="Local-helpers" tabindex="-1">Local helpers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Local-helpers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Local helpers {#Local-helpers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A local named helper with plain positional arguments (<code>add!(i, value) = push!(out, i * value)</code>) reads the locals of the code that defines it and writes their collections. It reads their values at each call, so a local reassigned before the call is read as reassigned. A helper that rebinds a captured local, has typed arguments or several methods, or is passed to a recorded helper while it captures locals, raises <code>JAXEmissionError</code>. Helpers that write their arguments in place are described in <a href="./jax-helpers">JAX helpers and submodels</a>.</p>',12)])])}const f=o(d,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
